cherry-pick and range notation.

26
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:54,909
The form is the one you know from Gates 1
and 2. One hundred points in four parts:

27
00:01:54,949 --> 00:01:59,864
Concepts 30, Prediction 20, Hands-on
diagnosis 30, Oral interview 20.

28
00:01:59,904 --> 00:02:05,680
The pass rule is the threshold overall and
at least 70 percent in every part: 21,

29
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:11,713
14, 21 and 14. A total above the threshold
with one part below 70 percent is a miss.

30
00:02:12,053 --> 00:02:14,030
Two things are particular to this gate.

31
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:17,951
First, the hands-on repository
is in the middle of something.

32
00:02:17,991 --> 00:02:22,878
A merge, a rebase, a cherry-pick or a revert
has been started and not finished, and

33
00:02:22,918 --> 00:02:26,500
the report that comes with it
is incomplete and partly wrong.

34
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:31,486
Your first job is not to fix. It's to read
which operation is in progress, and where

35
00:02:31,526 --> 00:02:32,140
it stopped.

36
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:37,194
Second, the scoring of the path. The
hands-on points are split between the end

37
00:02:37,234 --> 00:02:40,303
state, the safety of the
path, and the explanation.

38
00:02:40,343 --> 00:02:45,423
The assessments file says it directly: a
correct end state reached through git reset

39
00:02:45,463 --> 00:02:49,446
--hard, a forced push or a re-clone
can still fail the safety row.

40
00:02:49,486 --> 00:02:53,347
In this block you learned a safer
alternative for each of those.

41
00:02:53,387 --> 00:02:55,847
The gate checks whether you reach for it.

42
00:02:56,187 --> 00:03:01,110
One table carries most of the gate: for
each of merge, rebase step, cherry-pick and

43
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:04,996
revert, which commit is the base,
which is ours, which is theirs.

44
00:03:06,836 --> 00:03:08,988
There is one rule behind the whole table.

45
00:03:09,328 --> 00:03:13,007
"Ours" is HEAD, Git's name for
where you are. It's stage 2.

46
00:03:13,047 --> 00:03:16,474
It's the upper half of the
markers. That never changes.

47
00:03:16,814 --> 00:03:21,073
What changes between the four operations
is what HEAD is when the merge runs, and

48
00:03:21,113 --> 00:03:22,560
which commit plays the base.

49
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:27,793
In a merge, HEAD is your branch. In a
rebase, HEAD is the upstream plus the copies

50
00:03:27,833 --> 00:03:31,402
made so far, because step 2 of
a rebase detached HEAD there.

51
00:03:31,442 --> 00:03:35,011
In a cherry-pick and in a revert,
HEAD is your branch again.

52
00:03:35,351 --> 00:03:38,683
And the base: for a merge, the
merge base of the two tips.

53
00:03:38,723 --> 00:03:43,102
For a rebase step and a cherry-pick,
the parent of the commit being applied.

54
00:03:43,142 --> 00:03:47,869
For a revert, the commit being reverted
itself, with its parent as theirs, so that

55
00:03:47,909 --> 00:03:49,381
the change runs backwards.

56
00:03:49,721 --> 00:03:52,798
If you forget a cell in the
exam room, don't guess.

57
00:03:52,838 --> 00:03:54,938
Derive it from those two sentences.

58
00:03:55,278 --> 00:04:00,113
Quick quiz, three options. In a stopped
rebase, "theirs" is: option one, the

59
00:04:00,153 --> 00:04:04,475
upstream. Option two, your own
commit. Option three, the merge base.

60
00:04:04,515 --> 00:04:05,502
Say it out loud.

61
00:04:11,842 --> 00:04:16,447
Option two: in a rebase, theirs is your
own commit, because ours, HEAD, is the

62
00:04:16,487 --> 00:04:19,664
upstream side. Now check your
paper against the table.

63
00:04:21,504 --> 00:04:27,238
labs/run ch09/rebase-conflict:
a rebase stopped at a conflict.

64
00:04:32,378 --> 00:04:37,685
Stage 2, "ours", is HEAD: the upstream
commits plus what has been replayed so far.

65
00:04:37,725 --> 00:04:41,793
Stage 3, "theirs", is the commit
being replayed: your own work.

66
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:47,010
That's row two of the table. Now the same
two branches as a merge, run from your

67
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:51,444
branch. Predict whose value is on
top, under HEAD: yours, or main's?

68
00:04:51,484 --> 00:04:52,096
I'll wait.

69
00:04:56,936 --> 00:05:02,024
Yours. The same two branches as a merge,
run from your branch: the same two values

70
00:05:02,064 --> 00:05:04,401
in opposite positions. That's row one.

71
00:05:04,741 --> 00:05:09,937
labs/run ch10/pick-conflict: a
cherry-pick stopped at a conflict.

72
00:05:10,277 --> 00:05:13,840
The merge base of the two branches
has one version of the line.

73
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:17,958
Stage 1 has another, because stage 1
is the parent of the picked commit.

74
00:05:17,998 --> 00:05:19,902
That's the base cell of row three.

75
00:05:20,242 --> 00:05:25,307
For Part 3, after the generator has printed
the sandbox path and you have opened the

76
00:05:25,347 --> 00:05:30,168
